**Proposition 8.1 (credited full-family construction and exact alphabet).**
For a graph above, let $H_0=\lceil\log_2n\rceil$,
$K_0=2^{H_0}$ and $D=H_0+3$. The construction of [GDB, Theorem
17.1 and Convention 17.3][GDB] gives a lawful complete code for
every $t\in\mathcal F_D$ with exactly

$$
|S_D|-2^{2n}+1
\tag{WBF.33}
$$

attained names. Its one merged canonical fiber consists of all graph
tuples of Convention 1.5 in the first $n$ positions of a public
perfect height-$H_0$ right-subtree scaffold, with alpha padding in
the remaining $K_0-n$ positions. Every other canonical state is
assigned its own singleton name. The full fibers on original sources
are the inverse images under $\pi_D$, retaining every variable shape.

On every original source, before the later target is known,
preparation has the exact Read and literal charges

$$
T_{\rm prep}=2^D-1,\qquad
Q_{\rm prep}=D,\qquad
A_{\rm prep}=(D-1)2^D+1,
\tag{WBF.34}
$$

where $Q_{\rm prep}$ is the maximum submitted address length and
$A_{\rm prep}$ the total submitted letters. Every later request
uses at most one fresh original-root Read of the same stationary source,
with the address selected before its reply and any authentic count.

Proof and resource correspondence. The graph's $2^{2n}$ actual
tuples embed injectively into the ordered scaffold in $S_D$; each
perfect graph block belongs to $S_3$. The construction is the
credited GDB realization, with the following explicit mapping of
resources. A deterministic source-blind preparer scans all literal
words $Rq$, $q\in W_D$, in fixed public order, including absent
replies. Those paid replies determine $\pi_D(v)$. A finite public
membership table selects its merged or singleton name. Canonical
trees and this finite table are effectively enumerable; there is no
efficient-generation assertion. Every canonical state is itself an
actual right subtree of a source in $\mathcal F_D$. Thus all names
are attained, and merging exactly $2^{2n}$ distinct states gives
(WBF.33), with no preassigned unacquired colors.

There are $\sum_{j=0}^{D-1}2^j=2^D-1$ scan words, of lengths
$j+1$. Their total length is
$\sum_{j=0}^{D-1}(j+1)2^j=(D-1)2^D+1$, proving (WBF.34).
The scan includes words of length $D$, giving its stated maximum.
Export only a fixed-width representation of the complete name.
Use the separately paid handoff and isolation in Definition 1.2
to remove every other acquired distinction, including membership
work, selectors, buffers, lengths, allocation, control, timing and
transcripts. The complete attained alphabet is therefore the one
in (WBF.33), rather than a count that ignores a surviving cut
descriptor or hidden seed vector.

At a singleton name, every requested factor is fixed, giving zero
Reads. At the merged name, a target strictly above the scaffold
frontier is branch. Within a graph block its root and two children
are branch. A terminal target for edge $a$ reads the actual tail
owner at the single original-root word

$$
R a_{\operatorname{tail}(a)}q_{\operatorname{owner}(a)},
\qquad |a_{\operatorname{tail}(a)}|=H_0,
\qquad |q_{\operatorname{owner}(a)}|=2.
\tag{WBF.35}
$$

This address has length $D$ and is determined by the complete name,
public graph tables, $D$ and the target, before its reply and every
supplied count. Alpha or beta determines exactly the requested
terminal factor. Padding has fixed alpha factors, including absent
suffixes. Every allowed request has right-subtree suffix length at
most $D-1=H_0+2$, so these cases exhaust all requests. A terminal
and its owner are occurrences of the same actual edge bit; there
is only one late Read, not an independent endpoint observation.

All prefixes used to identify actual scaffold blocks are present.
Pair homomorphism therefore transports their predicted replies to
the corresponding original-root literals. The exact presence premise
of `NativeDepthPatterns.original_present_transport` is met only at
